ngth if you're using  regularizers  don't worry if you didn't get that  okay so also notice that the hinge loss  is an upper bound on the zero loss so  this is cool because suppose you  optimize the hinge loss and you drive it  down driving down this thing is going to  start pushing on the zero loss  more or less and particularly if you get  this hinge loss of zero  then what is the zero loss well it's  also going to be zero so that's that's a  nice fact  so here's a minor digression there's a  lot of other loss functions here is the  logistic loss and we can just plot it on  this diagram and you see that the  logistic loss doesn't have this kink in  it it has a smooth transition between  something that's growing linearly to  something that fades away to zero  and the  the key property of the logistic loss is  that even if you were out here  so if you have a margin of 2  then you're classifying correctly and  the hinge loss would say you get zero  loss and you don't need to do anything  but the logistic loss is greedy it says  well you still have a little bit of a  loss and if you try to minimize the  logistic loss you're just going to try  to  keep on pushing this margin far out as  possible  so the logistic loss is differentiable  everywhere and smooth and it's nice and  it's typically known as logistic  regression because it has connections to  probability  okay so let's now go back to the hinge  loss  here is our friend the hinge loss  and here is the expression for the hinge  loss and remember it's the maximum of  two expressions  um this decreasing align part and then  the zero part in orange and blue  respectively  okay so now if we want to apply gradient  descent to the hinge loss we have to  take the gradient  so how do we take the gradient so the  gradient of the loss  hinge is equal to  and now we have this max thing okay  which might be a little bit scary but if  you look up here  we can just do this kind of visually so  what is the slope here well the slope  here is whatever the slope of the orange  part is and what is the slope here it's  the slope of this  blue part  and so now we just have to switch  between the two cases  so in particular if  the margin  is orange apart  is greater than zero  that means we're in this region  and then the gradient is just going to  be the gradient of this expression one's  a constant it's so it's going to be  minus  we're differentiating with respect to w  so and phi of x y is a constant so it's  just going to be phi of x y  and then if this condition doesn't hold  otherwise that means we're in this  region  and what is the gradient of zero well  that's the world's easiest differential  uh calculus problem and it's uh zero  okay so this is the gradient of the  hinge loss  and just to kind of sanity check things  so if you have a pick up an example  and it's on this side over here then the  gradient is going to be zero and you're  not going to update your weights  on the other hand if you are over here  then the gradient will be non-zero in  particular it's going to be minus v of x  y  okay so now let's put things together  and revisit our  example so here's the purple classifier  over here  and  here we have some training data and  we're going to try to compute the hinge  loss on this training data along with  its gradient  okay so  remember the hinge loss is this  expression  so let's look at the first point 0 2  0 2 is here and it's labeled as a  positive so if you go and you plug  that point into the hinge loss then you  get a max over one minus the margin  and zero so what is the margin here  well it's this dot product which happens  to be two  so we have 1 minus 2 minus 1  and my max of minus 1 and 0 is 0. and  that agrees with our intuition that the  loss here should be 0 because it's  correctly classified and correctly  classified by a margin of 2.  and now let's look at the second point  minus 2  0  so if we compute the loss here  we see that the loss is actually 2.  so notice that even though we are  getting  this oh so this loss is actually 2  and that makes sense because we  misclassified this point  so now if we look at the third  point here  minus 1  sorry 1 minus 1  then the loss on this example is 1.5  so notice that even though we're  classifying this point correctly we're  still incurring a lot because the margin  was only 0.5 and didn't meet the  threshold  okay so  or i guess the loss is maybe 1.5  sorry the margin is 0.5 but the loss is  1.5  so  now we can also compute the gradients  here so the loss on the first  a point is zero because the loss is zero  and generally  not always if the loss is zero then the  gradient will be zero as well  and on the second  one the loss is not zero so we have a  non-zero gradient which is  minus of e of x  y so it's this  part  um  times this minus sign  and the third  point  also has positive loss so it has a  positive gradient 1 minus 1.  now  we can compute the train loss which is  the average over the losses that gives  us 1.17 and the gradient of the train  loss is just the average of the  gradients and that gives us 1 minus 0.33  okay so  let us now move on let's concretize this  in python  okay so let's uh remember last time  we coded up gradient descent for  uh  linear regression so now i'm gonna just  copy this  um  object against that send hinge  and do it for the hinge loss okay so i'm  going to use this as a starting point  and  i'm going to just change a few things um  let's  change the training examples because now  we're working with this training data  so we have uh just to keep track of  things so this is x y pairs  so x now  is um  zero two  one  and then the second point is minus two  zero  and the third point is one minus one  and so we have three points x y where x  is uh uh  triple  okay so phi  is uh just going to be  um  you know x  and  uh  the wave dimension of the white vector  is still two  and now the key thing we have to do is  change the uh the definition of the loss  so now let us see so we before we have  the average over a sum here and instead  of the square loss i'm going to make  this the hinge loss so the hinge loss is  max over 1 minus the margin  okay and 0.  all right so this is max over one minus  the margin and zero  and the gradient of that  um is going to be  so  um let's actually just copy this down  let's delete this  to not confuse ourselves  um so remember if the  this first expression  is greater than 0  then the gradient is minus  phi of x times y  if  we're on that side of  the curve and otherwise it's just going  to be  zero  okay so that's it we just changed the  training examples and changed  the  definition of the loss function  and  the optimization algorithm we don't  actually have to change at all  okay  so let's run this and see what we get  okay so here's where you said it starts  out with w equals zero and then it  starts moving w to  0.5 uh minus 0.55  you see that the the train loss is  decreasing nicely and actually in this  case it gets to  zero which means that we  remember the hinge loss is upper bound  on zero and loss so that means the zero  loss is also zero  and the gradient also vanishes  and becomes zero meaning that we  converged  okay so just to recap all we did here  was change the training examples the  feature riser  and uh redefine the loss and it's great  that we didn't have to touch the  optimization algorithm because this was  meant to be a generic piece of code  all right so  let us summarize  and in particular i'm going to contrast  regression with classification since  we've seen two of them so far  so  the key  quantity  that drives the prediction in both cases  is the score the dot product between the  weight vector and the feature vector  and in regression the prediction is  exactly just a raw score while in  classification you stick it through the  sine function so you get a one  or a minus one  how the prediction is related to the  target  well in regression we looked at the  residual which was the score minus y  and in classification we're looking at  the margin so in regression low residual  was good  in classification high margin is good  because we want score and y to have the  same sign  using those quantities we can define  loss functions  so in regression we looked at the square  laws but as i mentioned briefly you can  also do the absolute deviation loss in  classification the story becomes a  little bit stranger  because we generally care about the zero  loss that's our misclassification rate  but we can't optimize it so we have to  come up with a surrogate loss function  like the hinge loss which we went into  depth and the logistic loss which we  briefly mentioned  and given the loss functions  in both cases we use the gradient  descent algorithm to optimize the loss  function  that's it that concludes the unit on  linear classification thanks for  listening
 

